          title: "Why Atoms Stick: The Bonding Zoo",
          content: `
<p>Before asking how atoms arrange themselves, ask why they condense at all. Every solid is held together by electromagnetism plus quantum mechanics, but the compromise between electron kinetic energy, Coulomb attraction, and the Pauli principle plays out in several distinct styles — and the style predicts the material's personality.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ionic bonding</strong> (NaCl): one atom donates an electron, the other accepts, and the resulting + and − ions attract. Strong, non-directional → hard, brittle, transparent crystals with high melting points that dissolve in water and conduct only when molten.</li>
  <li><strong>Covalent bonding</strong> (diamond, silicon): neighboring atoms <em>share</em> electrons in overlapping orbitals. Strongly <em>directional</em> — carbon insists on its tetrahedral 109.5° angles — giving rigid, hard crystals and, crucially for this subject, semiconductors.</li>
  <li><strong>Metallic bonding</strong> (Cu, Na, Fe): outer electrons delocalize completely, forming a negatively charged sea that glues the positive ion cores together. The sea explains everything metallic: electrical and thermal conduction, shininess, and ductility — ions can slide past each other without breaking directed bonds.</li>
  <li><strong>Van der Waals bonding</strong> (solid argon, the layers of graphite): fluctuating dipoles attract weakly. Soft solids, low melting points.</li>
  <li><strong>Hydrogen bonding</strong> (ice): a hydrogen shared between electronegative atoms — the reason ice floats and proteins fold.</li>
</ul>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>Bonding is a competition</h4>
  <p>Squeezing electrons into small spaces costs kinetic energy (the uncertainty principle from Subject 26); letting them spread near several nuclei saves it. Solids form because some arrangement of nuclei lets the electrons strike a better energy bargain than isolated atoms could — typically about 1–10 eV per atom, the <strong>cohesive energy</strong>.</p>
</div>
<p>And why do the atoms then line up <em>periodically</em>? Because whatever local arrangement minimizes the energy for one atom's neighborhood minimizes it for every identical atom — so the optimal pattern repeats. Periodicity is not an assumption but an outcome, and it is spectacularly consequential: nearly everything in the next five chapters (diffraction, phonons, Bloch's theorem, band gaps) flows from translational symmetry alone.</p>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>Graphite and diamond are both pure carbon. Strong covalent sheets bound by feeble van der Waals forces make graphite soft enough for pencils; the same bonds arranged in a 3D tetrahedral network make diamond the hardest natural material. Structure, not chemistry, is destiny.</p>
</div>`,
