          content: `
<p>Here is the idea that unlocks the entire subject. Everything physical inside a crystal — electron density, potential energy — is periodic: f(r + R) = f(r) for every lattice vector R. Subject 23 taught that periodic functions are sums of plane waves, but only <em>special</em> plane waves fit a given periodicity. A wave e<sup>iG·r</sup> respects the lattice only if</p>
<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.1em;"><strong>e<sup>iG·R</sup> = 1, i.e. G·R = 2π × integer, for every lattice vector R.</strong></p>
<p>The set of all wavevectors G that pass this test is itself a lattice — in k-space — called the <strong>reciprocal lattice</strong>. It is, quite literally, the Fourier transform of the crystal: the direct lattice says where atoms sit; the reciprocal lattice says which waves know about it. Its primitive vectors b₁, b₂, b₃ obey aᵢ·bⱼ = 2πδᵢⱼ, so big real-space spacings mean small reciprocal-space spacings and vice versa — hence "reciprocal."</p>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>Planes ↔ points</h4>
  <p>Each reciprocal lattice vector G is perpendicular to a family of Miller-index lattice planes, and the shortest such G has magnitude |G| = 2π/d, where d is the spacing of those planes. One point in reciprocal space encodes an entire stack of planes in real space. This dictionary is exactly what diffraction reads out.</p>
</div>
<p>Two facts to file away. First, a pleasing duality: the reciprocal lattice of fcc is bcc, and vice versa (sc is its own reciprocal). Second, the <strong>Brillouin zone</strong>: the region of k-space closer to G = 0 than to any other reciprocal lattice point — the Wigner–Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice. Because adding any G to a wavevector changes nothing physical about a lattice wave, all distinct wave physics in a crystal lives inside this one zone. Phonon dispersions (next chapter) and electron band structures (chapter three) are always plotted across it.</p>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>Experimentalists live so completely in reciprocal space that a diffraction pattern feels more "real" to them than the atomic positions. A crystal's symmetry, orientation, and quality can all be judged at a glance in k-space — real space is what you reconstruct afterwards, by inverse Fourier transform.</p>
</div>`,
