          title: "Balls and Springs: The One-Dimensional Chain",
          content: `
<p>Solid state physics' favorite toy model: an infinite chain of identical atoms, mass m, spacing a, joined by springs of stiffness κ. Displace atom n by uₙ and Newton's second law reads m·üₙ = κ(uₙ₊₁ + uₙ₋₁ − 2uₙ) — each atom pulled toward the average of its neighbors. Try a wave solution uₙ = A e<sup>i(kna − ωt)</sup> and the equation collapses to the chain's <strong>dispersion relation</strong>:</p>
<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.15em;"><strong>ω(k) = 2√(κ/m) · |sin(ka/2)|</strong></p>
<p>This one formula repays study. At small k (wavelengths long compared with a), sin(ka/2) ≈ ka/2 and ω ≈ vk with v = a√(κ/m): the chain carries ordinary <strong>sound</strong>, with a speed set by stiffness over mass — the same √(spring/inertia) logic as every oscillator since Subject 8. The discreteness of the atoms is invisible to long waves, which is why continuum elasticity works so well.</p>
<p>Near the zone boundary k = π/a the curve flattens and the <strong>group velocity dω/dk drops to zero</strong>. There the wavelength is 2a: adjacent atoms swing in perfect antiphase, forming a standing wave that Bragg-reflects off the lattice itself and transports no energy. A discrete chain also has a maximum frequency, 2√(κ/m) — shake one end faster and the disturbance simply refuses to propagate.</p>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>The Brillouin zone strikes again</h4>
  <p>Shift k by 2π/a and uₙ = A e^(ikna) is unchanged at every atom — between the atoms there is nothing to wave. Wavevectors differing by a reciprocal lattice vector are physically identical, so every distinct vibration lives in the first Brillouin zone, −π/a &lt; k ≤ π/a. A finite chain of N atoms holds exactly N allowed k-values in that zone: N atoms, N modes. Count conserved.</p>
</div>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>Plug in real numbers for copper — atomic mass, interatomic spring constants inferred from elastic moduli — and v = a√(κ/m) lands within a few percent of copper's measured sound speed of about 4700 m/s. Two atoms and a spring already know how fast sound travels in a metal.</p>
</div>`,
