          content: `
<p>Everything so far was classical. Now recall Subject 26: each normal mode of the chain is mathematically a harmonic oscillator, and a quantum harmonic oscillator of frequency ω cannot hold arbitrary energy — only the rungs of a ladder:</p>
<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.1em;"><strong>E = ħω(n + ½),&nbsp;&nbsp;n = 0, 1, 2, …</strong></p>
<p>Quantizing every mode of the lattice therefore chops vibrational energy into lumps of ħω. Each lump behaves like a particle: it carries energy ħω, crystal momentum ħk, and can be created, destroyed, and scattered. We call it a <strong>phonon</strong> — a particle of sound, the exact acoustic analogue of the photon. A hot crystal is a box full of phonon gas.</p>
<p>Because any number of phonons can pile into the same mode, phonons are <strong>bosons</strong>, and in thermal equilibrium the average number in a mode of frequency ω follows the Bose–Einstein distribution:</p>
<p style="text-align:center;">⟨n⟩ = 1/(e<sup>ħω/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1)</p>
<p>Read its two limits — they run the next section. When k<sub>B</sub>T ≫ ħω, ⟨n⟩ ≈ k<sub>B</sub>T/ħω: the mode holds energy k<sub>B</sub>T, the classical equipartition answer, and quantum mechanics hides. When k<sub>B</sub>T ≪ ħω, ⟨n⟩ ≈ e<sup>−ħω/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup>: the mode is <strong>frozen out</strong>, too expensive to excite even once, and contributes essentially nothing to the thermal energy.</p>
<div class="analogy">
  <h3>Analogy: The Vending Machine</h3>
  <p>A mode of frequency ω is a vending machine whose cheapest item costs ħω, and temperature is your pocket money k<sub>B</sub>T. Rich compared to the price, you buy freely and never notice items have discrete prices — that is the classical limit. Poor compared to the price, you almost never buy at all. There is no option to purchase half an item: that is quantization, and it is the entire resolution of the heat-capacity crisis coming next.</p>
</div>
<p>Phonons also do real work in the lab and in your laptop. They are the dominant carriers of heat in insulators, they scatter electrons (a major source of electrical resistance, as chapter three will show), and — a promissory note for the final chapter — exchanging phonons is what glues electrons into the Cooper pairs behind superconductivity.</p>`,
