          title: "The Free-Electron Gas and the Fermi Sea",
          content: `
<p>Start with the boldest possible approximation: in a metal, ignore the ions entirely and treat the conduction electrons as free particles in a box. Classically (Drude's 1900 model) this explains Ohm's law and why good electrical conductors are good thermal conductors — but it also predicts each electron should add (3/2)k<sub>B</sub>T of thermal energy, an electronic heat capacity that simply is not there. Metals stubbornly obey Dulong–Petit as if their electrons were thermally invisible.</p>
<p>Sommerfeld's 1927 fix imports one quantum fact: electrons are <strong>fermions</strong>, and the Pauli principle allows at most two (spin up, spin down) per momentum state. Fill the box's plane-wave states from the bottom up with all N electrons and you build the <strong>Fermi sea</strong>: every state filled up to a sharp surface in k-space, the <strong>Fermi surface</strong> at wavevector k<sub>F</sub>, and empty above. The topmost filling energy is the <strong>Fermi energy</strong>:</p>
<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.05em;">ε<sub>F</sub> = ħ²k<sub>F</sub>²/2m,&nbsp;&nbsp;with k<sub>F</sub> = (3π²n)<sup>1/3</sup></p>
<p>Because metals are dense in electrons (n ≈ 10²⁹ per m³), ε<sub>F</sub> is enormous: about 7 eV for copper, equivalent to a temperature T<sub>F</sub> = ε<sub>F</sub>/k<sub>B</sub> ≈ 80,000 K. Room temperature is a 0.4% ripple on that. Electrons at the Fermi surface race at about 1.6 × 10⁶ m/s — <em>at absolute zero</em>, forced to move by Pauli alone.</p>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>Only the surface is alive</h4>
  <p>An electron deep in the sea cannot absorb a k<sub>B</sub>T-sized nudge — every nearby state is occupied. Only the thin shell within about k<sub>B</sub>T of the Fermi surface, a fraction ≈ T/T<sub>F</sub> of all electrons, can respond to heat, fields, or collisions. That single idea explains the missing heat capacity (suppressed by T/T<sub>F</sub>, leaving a small contribution linear in T that dominates only below a few kelvin) and runs quietly beneath everything metals do.</p>
</div>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>The same "degenerate fermion" physics reappears at cosmic scale: a white dwarf star is held up against gravity by electron degeneracy pressure — the Fermi sea's refusal to be compressed. Subject 32 collapses that support into neutron stars. A copper wire and a dead star share their equation of state's logic.</p>
</div>`,
