          id: "band-models",
          title: "Two Roads to Bands: Nearly Free and Tight Binding",
          content: `
<p>How do energy bands and gaps actually arise? Two caricatures, starting from opposite extremes, converge on the same answer — which is how you know the answer is robust.</p>
<p><strong>Road 1: nearly-free electrons.</strong> Start from free electrons, ε = ħ²k²/2m, and switch on a <em>weak</em> periodic potential. For most k the electron barely notices. But at the Brillouin zone boundary k = ±π/a, the electron's wavelength satisfies Bragg's condition — the lattice reflects the wave back on itself, exactly like the X-rays of chapter one. Forward and reflected waves combine into two standing waves: one piling its probability density <em>on</em> the ion cores (lowered energy), one piling it <em>between</em> them (raised energy). Traveling solutions at that k cease to exist, and the free parabola splits: an <strong>energy gap</strong> opens at every zone boundary, of width set by the corresponding Fourier component of the lattice potential. Bands are free-electron physics interrupted by Bragg reflection.</p>
<p><strong>Road 2: tight binding.</strong> Start from the opposite limit — isolated atoms, each electron locked in an atomic orbital of sharp energy. Bring N atoms together and orbitals on neighboring atoms overlap slightly, letting electrons <strong>hop</strong> with amplitude t. Each sharp atomic level then fans out into a <strong>band</strong> of N closely spaced levels; for a 1D chain, ε(k) = ε<sub>atomic</sub> − 2t cos(ka), a band of width 4t. Strong overlap (outer orbitals) makes wide bands and mobile electrons; weak overlap (core orbitals) makes narrow bands and homebody electrons. Gaps here are just the leftover spacing between one atomic level's band and the next.</p>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>Same skeleton from both ends</h4>
  <p>Weak potential or strong, the outcome is identical in structure: allowed <strong>bands</strong> of N states each (2N counting spin), separated by forbidden <strong>gaps</strong>, with ε(k) periodic across the Brillouin zone and flat at its edges. Real materials sit between the caricatures, and modern density-functional codes compute their bands numerically — but every computed band structure is read with these two intuitions.</p>
</div>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>Near a band edge, ε(k) is approximately parabolic — so the electron responds to forces like a free particle with an <strong>effective mass</strong> m* set by the band's curvature. In gallium arsenide m* is 0.067 electron masses; band-edge electrons there accelerate 15 times more eagerly than free ones. Materials engineering is, to a first approximation, effective-mass shopping.</p>
</div>`,
