          id: "dia-para",
          title: "Diamagnets and Paramagnets",
          content: `
<p>Apply a magnetic field to matter and matter answers. The size and sign of the answer — the <strong>susceptibility</strong> χ, defined by magnetization M = χH — sorts all materials into a few great families, and each family is a quantum story.</p>
<p><strong>Diamagnetism: the universal, reluctant response.</strong> Every atom's electron clouds adjust to an applied field in the direction Lenz's law demands — inducing currents whose magnetic moment <em>opposes</em> the field. All matter is therefore weakly diamagnetic (χ ≈ −10⁻⁵), and the effect is temperature-independent. It is feeble but real: water is diamagnetic, which is why a sufficiently strong field can levitate a frog — famously demonstrated in a 16-tesla magnet. A deep footnote: classical physics actually forbids any equilibrium magnetism at all (the Bohr–van Leeuwen theorem) — every χ in this chapter, even humble diamagnetism, is quantum mechanical.</p>
<p><strong>Paramagnetism: spins that answer the call.</strong> Atoms with unpaired electrons carry permanent magnetic moments (spin, Subject 26's most stubborn quantum fact). A field favors alignment by energy ~μB; thermal agitation randomizes. The competition gives a positive susceptibility that grows as temperature falls:</p>
<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.05em;"><strong>χ = C/T&nbsp;&nbsp;(Curie's law)</strong></p>
<p>At room temperature the ordering is minuscule — μB/k<sub>B</sub>T ≈ 10⁻³ even at 1 tesla — so paramagnets are only faintly magnetic. Aluminum, oxygen, and most rare-earth salts behave this way.</p>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>Alignment versus agitation</h4>
  <p>Curie's 1/T law is the same fight you have seen since thermal physics: an ordering energy (here μB) against k<sub>B</sub>T of disorder, with Boltzmann as referee. Cool a paramagnet and it orders more easily — which raises the question this chapter turns on: can the spins ever decide to align <em>without</em> any applied field? For iron, the answer is yes, and the reason is not what 19th-century physics guessed.</p>
</div>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>Liquid oxygen is paramagnetic enough to hang visibly between the poles of a strong magnet — a classroom-famous demonstration that O₂ has two unpaired electrons, a fact that baffled early chemical bonding theory and helped motivate molecular orbital theory.</p>
</div>`,
