          title: "Zero Resistance and the Meissner Effect",
          content: `
<p>In 1911, three years after liquefying helium, Kamerlingh Onnes cooled mercury to 4.2 K and watched its resistance fall — not smoothly, but off a cliff: to zero. Not small; <strong>zero</strong>. Currents started in superconducting rings circulate for years with no measurable decay (persistent-current experiments bound the decay time above 100,000 years). Below its critical temperature T<sub>c</sub>, a superconductor is a new thermodynamic phase of the electrons, as sharply distinct from a metal as ice is from water.</p>
<p>The deeper surprise came in 1933. Meissner and Ochsenfeld found that a superconductor <strong>expels magnetic field from its interior</strong> — and does so even when cooled <em>in</em> a pre-existing field. That second clause is the crucial one. A merely perfect conductor would trap whatever flux threaded it at the transition (induced eddy currents oppose any <em>change</em>). Expelling an already-present field is not perfect conduction; it is an equilibrium property: B = 0 inside is what the superconducting phase <em>wants</em>. This <strong>Meissner effect</strong> is what levitates magnets above superconductors, and it certifies superconductivity as a genuine phase of matter, not just resistance-free metal.</p>
<p>The brothers London captured both facts phenomenologically in 1935. Treating the supercurrent as an electron fluid that responds to fields without scattering, their equations imply that magnetic field does not stop dead at the surface but decays exponentially inside:</p>
<p style="text-align:center; font-size:1.05em;"><strong>B(x) = B(0) e<sup>−x/λ<sub>L</sub></sup></strong></p>
<p>with the <strong>London penetration depth</strong> λ<sub>L</sub> typically 50–500 nm. Screening supercurrents flowing in that thin skin generate exactly the field needed to cancel the applied one everywhere deeper. Strong enough fields (or currents) destroy the state — every superconductor has a critical field, which is why magnet engineering is a battle of materials.</p>
<div class="did-you-know">
  <h4>Did you know?</h4>
  <p>Superconductivity is already industrial: MRI machines image bodies inside multi-tesla fields from persistent supercurrents in niobium–titanium coils that are charged once and then sealed. The LHC steers protons with 1200 superconducting magnets, and experimental maglev trains in Japan levitate on the Meissner effect at 600 km/h.</p>
</div>
<div class="key-concept">
  <h4>What still needs explaining</h4>
  <p>London theory describes; it does not explain. Why do electrons — mutually repelling fermions in a dirty lattice — suddenly organize into a scatter-proof fluid at low temperature? That question defeated Einstein, Bohr, Heisenberg, and Feynman, and stood for 46 years. Its resolution is next.</p>
</div>`,
